course: everyday
lesson: 25
title: Snakes, spiders and scorpions
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Some places have venomous creatures: rattlesnakes and bark scorpions in Arizona and Texas, black widow spiders across much of the USA, brown snakes, redback and funnel-web spiders in Australia, and the adder in Britain.
explain: These animals do not want to fight a person; most bites happen when someone steps on one, puts a hand where they cannot see, or tries to catch, poke or kill it.
explain: The rule is the same everywhere: if you see one, stop, back away slowly, never touch it, even if it looks dead, and tell an adult.
explain: Prevention is simple: stay on paths, wear shoes and long trousers in the bush or desert, look before you put hands under rocks and logs, and shake out shoes and clothes left outside.
explain: If someone is bitten, call the emergency number, keep them calm and still and keep the bitten arm or leg still; never cut the bite, suck it or tie it tight with a cord.
explain: In Australia, adults are taught to wrap a firm pressure bandage over a snake or funnel-web bite and the whole limb and keep it still, because this slows the venom moving through the body.
explain: A man in Queensland tried to pick up a brown snake to move it; it bit him, and he needed antivenom in hospital.
explain: A boy in Arizona put on a boot left outside overnight without shaking it; a scorpion inside stung his foot, and it hurt for hours.
fact: Venomous snakes and spiders usually bite only when they are stepped on, touched or cornered.
fact: Rattlesnakes live in Arizona, Texas and many other parts of the USA, and they do not always rattle before they strike.
fact: A snake that looks dead can still bite by reflex, so it must never be touched.
fact: If you see a snake, stop, back away slowly and give it plenty of room to leave.
fact: Never put your hands into places you cannot see, such as under rocks, logs or into holes.
fact: Shaking out shoes, gloves and clothes left outside stops a hidden scorpion or spider from stinging you.
fact: After a venomous bite, call the emergency number: 911 in the USA and Canada, 000 in Australia, 999 in the UK.
fact: Keep a bitten person calm and still, because moving around helps the venom spread.
fact: Never cut a bite, suck out venom or tie a tight cord around the limb.
fact: In Australia, a firm pressure bandage and keeping the limb still is the first aid for snake and funnel-web spider bites.
fact: The adder is the only venomous snake that lives wild in Britain, and its bite needs a hospital visit.
fact: Ireland and New Zealand have no wild land snakes.
fact: Many snake bites happen when people try to catch, move or kill the snake.
fact: A snake bite is an emergency: keep the person still and call for help at once.
exercise: You see a rattlesnake on a trail in Arizona. What should you do? | Stop, back away slowly and tell an adult | Run past it as fast as you can before it moves | Poke it with a stick to move it | Throw a stone to scare it off
exercise: Why do most snake bites happen? | Someone steps on, touches or tries to catch the snake | Snakes hunt people for food | Snakes chase anyone they see | Snakes attack people who stand still for too long
exercise: Does a rattlesnake always rattle before it bites? | No, it can strike without rattling | Yes, it always rattles three times first | Yes, it cannot bite without rattling | No, rattlesnakes never rattle at all
exercise: A snake on the road looks dead. Why must you still not touch it? | It can still bite by reflex | Dead snakes turn into live ones | It will be too cold to touch | Its skin will stick to your hand
exercise: Why should you shake out your shoes in the morning when camping in Texas? | A scorpion or spider may be hiding inside | Shoes fill up with dew overnight | It warms the shoes up | It makes the laces untie
exercise: Where should you never put your hands in the bush or desert? | Under rocks and logs or into holes you cannot see | On your own backpack straps | In your pockets | On a smooth, open path
exercise: Someone is bitten by a snake. What should they do while help comes? | Stay calm and keep the bitten limb still | Run to the car as fast as they can | Cut the bite open to let the venom out | Suck the venom out with their mouth and spit
exercise: Why is it bad for someone with a venomous bite to walk around a lot? | Moving helps the venom spread through the body | Walking makes the bite itch more and more | It will make them too hungry | Venom only works on people lying down
exercise: Which number do you call for a snake bite in Australia? | 000 | 911 | 999 | 111
exercise: What is the first aid for a funnel-web spider bite in Australia? | A firm pressure bandage and keeping the limb still | Ice cubes rubbed straight on the bite | A tight cord tied above the bite to stop the venom | Washing it in very hot water
exercise: Which is the only venomous snake living wild in Britain? | The adder | The rattlesnake | The cobra | The black mamba
exercise: Which country has no wild land snakes? | New Zealand | Australia | The USA | The whole of Canada
exercise: What should you wear when walking through long grass where snakes live? | Closed shoes and long trousers | Bare feet so you can feel the ground | Flip-flops and shorts | Socks without shoes
exercise: Why should you stay on the path when hiking in snake country? | You can see where you step and avoid hidden snakes | Snakes are not allowed on paths | Paths are always warmer | Snakes are scared of path signs and the people who read them
exercise: Which of these should never be done to a snake bite? | Tying a tight cord around the limb | Keeping the person still | Calling the emergency number for help | Keeping the person calm
test: Ethan, a boy in Phoenix, wants to pick up a small snake to show his friends. Why is this a bad idea? | It may be venomous and bite when grabbed | Small snakes are always venomous | Snakes melt in warm hands | His friends will be scared of him for the rest of the year
test: A girl in Queensland sees a brown snake cross her garden path. Which plan is best? | Stay back, let it move on and tell an adult | Chase it away with a broom | Step over it quickly | Try to catch it in a bucket and take it far away
test: Which is true? | Most snakes would rather escape than bite | Snakes chase people to bite them | Snakes cannot bite if they are not rattling | All snakes are deadly
test: Noah's dad in Texas is bitten on the ankle by a rattlesnake. What should Noah do? | Call 911 and help his dad stay calm and still | Suck the venom out of the bite | Make his dad walk fast to the car | Tie a shoelace tightly round his leg above the bite
test: A family camps in Arizona and leaves boots outside the tent. What should they do in the morning? | Shake the boots out before putting them on | Put the boots straight on in the dark | Leave the boots outside for good | Wash the boots in the river
test: A boy in Cornwall is bitten by an adder on a heath. What should happen? | He keeps still and calm and is taken to hospital | He walks it off and finishes the hike | He washes it and goes home to bed to sleep it off | He waits to see if it gets worse
test: A tourist in Sydney wants to lift a log to look for spiders. What is the danger? | A funnel-web or other spider may bite her hand | Logs in Sydney are always too heavy | Spiders in Australia are all harmless, so there is no danger | Lifting a log will wake a koala
test: Why do Australians put a firm bandage over a snake bite and keep the limb still? | It slows the venom spreading through the body | It squeezes all the venom back out of the bite | It stops the bite from itching | It keeps the snake from coming back
test: Which family is least likely to meet a wild snake at home? | A family in Ireland | A family in Arizona | A family in Queensland | A family in Texas
test: hiking in arizona, there's a snake on the path | Stop, back away slowly, give it room | Poke it with a stick | Jump over it quickly | Pick it up and move it off the path
test: snake looks dead, can i touch it | No, it can still bite | Yes, dead snakes are safe | Yes, by the tail | Yes, with a glove
test: shoes were outside all night, just put em on | Shake them out first | Yes, just put them on | Only check the left one | Check them next week
test: friend got bitten by a snake, suck it out like in films? | No, keep him still and call for help | Yes, suck it out quickly before it spreads | Cut the bite open | Tie a cord tight above it
test: snake on the path, mate wants to catch it with a stick | No, back away slowly and leave it alone | Yes, if he holds it behind the head | Yes, if it looks small | Yes, if he is fast enough to grab it first
test: brother got bit by a snake in the garden!! | Keep him still and calm, call emergency services | Make him run to the car fast | Suck the bite to get the venom out | Cut the bite and wash it
test: shoes were outside all night in arizona, just put em on? | Shake them out first, a scorpion may be inside | Put them on quickly | Only check if they smell | Wear them with thick socks instead, so nothing can bite
test: heard a guy in Arizona picked up a dead rattlesnake for a photo and it bit him, the head was cut off and everything | Real lesson: even a dead-looking snake can bite by reflex, so never touch one | Real lesson: snakes are safe once their head is off | It proves only Arizona snakes are dangerous | It means you should take photos from closer
test: my cousin reckons if a snake bites u, u suck the poison out like in the films | Wrong; never cut or suck a bite, keep the person still and call the emergency number | He is right, sucking removes the venom | He is right, if you then tie a tight cord | He is right, but only for small snakes
test: they're saying Sienna's house has redbacks everywhere cos her family are messy, spread it? | No, it is a cruel rumour; spiders live in many houses and gardens | Yes, because spiders prove a house is dirty | Yes, so people stay away from her | Yes, and say one bit her sister too
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the circumstances in which venomous snakes and spiders commonly bite? | When they are stepped upon, touched or cornered | When a person walks past at a distance | Only in the hours of darkness | Without any provocation whatsoever
exam: It being given that a snake is observed upon a path, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | To stop, to withdraw slowly, and to allow the snake ample room to depart | To strike the snake with a stick | To approach the snake to determine its species | To throw stones until the snake departs
exam: Identify the precaution recommended in respect of shoes, gloves and clothing left out of doors. | They should be shaken out before being worn | They should be worn immediately to keep them warm | They should be sprayed with water before being worn | They should be left out for a further night
exam: Select the first aid that is consistent with established Australian guidance for a snake bite. | A firm pressure bandage, the limb being kept still | The cutting of the wound and the sucking out of the venom | A tight cord tied above the bite | Encouraging the person to walk to seek help
